aften 0.09pipe1 - change notes
==============================

Base: upstream aften, SourceForge git snapshot 89aee3d (Jan 2011,
"0.08 + git"), kept byte-identical.  The encoder, the bitstream, the
API and the PCM library are untouched; the handful of fixes below are
all that this fork adds.  Upstream authorship (Justin Ruggles, Prakash
Punnoor, et al.) is preserved in the file headers and in git history.

This is a personal, for-fun build for continued use of a great little
tool.  Upstream has been quiet since 2011, so this is not an attempt
to take over anything, and there is nothing to credit to anyone here.

Patches (see git log for the exact commits)
-------------------------------------------
1. pcm/wav.c  (wave_init)
   Streaming muxers (FFmpeg et al.) write 0xFFFFFFFF as placeholder
   data chunk size when the output is a pipe.  That value read into an
   int is -1, which sign-extends into a bogus data_size of
   0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF when assigned to the 64-bit field; the derived
   sample count overflowed into nonsense (broken progress display).
   On non-seekable input the placeholder is now treated as
   size-unknown: read to end-of-stream.  Streamed WAV input works
   without -readtoeof 1.

2. aften/opts.c
   stdin is auto-detected: if no input filename is given and stdin is
   not a terminal, "-" is used as input:
       ffmpeg -i in.wav -f wav - | aften out.ac3
       aften out.ac3 < in.wav
   Explicit "aften - out.ac3" works as it has since 2006.

3. aften/aften.c
   - stdin/stdout are no longer fclose()d on exit (MSVC debug CRT
     aborts on the second close at process teardown).
   - fixed memmove size when -pad 0 and the stream is shorter than
     256 samples (copied nr bytes instead of nr*channels*sizeof(FLOAT)).

4. aften/helptext.h
   Usage and longhelp document pipe I/O ("-" filenames).  Pipe input
   and output existed in aften since 2006 but were never mentioned in
   the help text anywhere, which is the reason the tool is widely
   believed to lack the feature.

5. libaften/a52.h, Changelog
   Version string: "git" -> "0.09pipe1".

Verification
------------
Pipe encodes (stdin, ffmpeg streaming pipe, stdout, 24-bit) are
byte-identical to the file-based encodes of the same audio, and to the
upstream encoder's output (single-threaded CBR; the encoder core is
unmodified, this just proves the I/O paths are lossless).
